      "headline": "New Oral Drug 'Enlicitide' Reduces LDL Cholesterol by 60% in Landmark Phase 3 Trial",
      "description": "A phase 3 clinical trial — the CORALreef Lipids trial — published in The New England Journal of Medicine demonstrated that enlicitide decanoate, an investigational once-daily oral PCSK9 inhibitor developed by Merck, reduced LDL cholesterol by approximately 60% compared to placebo in 2,909 adults with atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease or at elevated risk. The reduction was sustained at 52 weeks. Enlicitide also reduced non-HDL cholesterol by 53%, apolipoprotein B by 50%, and lipoprotein(a) by 28%. Seven out of ten participants achieved LDL below 70 mg/dL. The FDA has granted the drug a National Priority Voucher to fast-track approval.",
      "context": "Enlicitide is the first oral drug to match the efficacy of injectable PCSK9 inhibitors such as evolocumab (Repatha) and alirocumab (Praluent), which have been available since 2015 but are rarely prescribed by primary care physicians — partly because they require injection. Statins remain inadequate for nearly half of high-risk cardiovascular patients. An oral PCSK9 inhibitor that matches injectable efficacy could dramatically expand treatment uptake: approximately 86 million US adults have hypercholesterolemia, and 70% of high-risk patients currently fail to reach LDL targets even on statins. This represents a potential paradigm shift in population-level cardiovascular prevention comparable in scope to the original introduction of statins.",
