      "headline": "Shingles Vaccine Linked to 46% Reduction in Major Cardiac Events in High-Risk Patients",
      "description": "A study of 246,822 US adults aged 50 and older with atherosclerotic heart disease — analyzed from the TriNetX database (2018–2025) — found that those who received at least one dose of the shingles vaccine (Shingrix or Zostavax) were 46% less likely to experience a major adverse cardiac event within one year compared to matched unvaccinated controls. Vaccinated patients were also 66% less likely to die from any cause, 32% less likely to have a heart attack, and 25% less likely to suffer a stroke or develop heart failure. The study, led by Dr. Robert Nguyen of UC Riverside, will be presented at the American College of Cardiology Annual Scientific Session (ACC.26) in New Orleans, March 28–30.",
      "context": "The magnitude of cardiovascular risk reduction — comparable to the benefit of quitting smoking — is striking given that the shingles vaccine was developed solely to prevent herpes zoster (shingles). The proposed mechanism is that by preventing shingles infection, the vaccine also prevents the clot-promoting inflammation that shingles triggers around the heart and brain. With approximately 70% of high-risk cardiovascular patients failing to achieve LDL targets and many facing residual cardiac risk despite optimal drug therapy, adding a widely available vaccine to the prevention toolkit represents a potentially transformative, low-cost, population-level intervention. The findings reinforce CDC recommendations for universal shingles vaccination in adults over 50, particularly those with established heart disease.",
